{
  "_comment": [
    "The hub registry: a fictional 40-site regional distribution network, and the",
    "fixed portfolio this system ranks. Hand-authored configuration, not a database.",
    "",
    "WHY 40 AND NOT ALL ~3,200 US COUNTIES. The question this system answers is",
    "'which of OUR hubs should we prioritise for resilience investment', so the hub",
    "set is a business fact, not a geographic sweep. Ranking every county answers a",
    "different question, and would largely reproduce FEMA's own national percentile",
    "ranking with extra steps. 40 gives every census region 10 hubs, so a regional",
    "question ('which Midwest hubs are most exposed to winter disruption?') ranks a",
    "meaningful field rather than three items.",
    "",
    "latitude/longitude are approximate city centres. The exact point matters less",
    "than it appears: Open-Meteo resolves to the nearest 9-25 km reanalysis grid",
    "cell, so the snapshot records the returned coordinates alongside these",
    "requested ones and the delta is reported by scripts/verify_snapshots.py.",
    "",
    "THERE ARE TWO FIPS KEYS BECAUSE THE TWO UPSTREAMS DISAGREE. Connecticut",
    "abolished its counties for statistical purposes in 2022; FEMA NRI v1.20.0 has",
    "followed the Census Bureau onto the nine Council-of-Governments planning",
    "regions (Hartford is in Capitol Planning Region, 09110), while the NWS alert",
    "feed still issues against the legacy counties (CTC003 / 09003). One field",
    "named county_fips would therefore be correct for one upstream and silently",
    "wrong for the other, for one hub, in a way that looks like 'Hartford has no",
    "hazard baseline' or 'Hartford never has alerts'. So the join key for each",
    "upstream is named for that upstream and both are stated explicitly, even",
    "though they are identical for the other 39 hubs.",
    "",
    "  nri_fips         -> FEMA NRI STCOFIPS",
    "  nws_county_fips  -> NWS alerts, via the SAME code (see app/config.same_to_fips)",
    "",
    "Every nri_fips is validated against the live NRI service by",
    "scripts/fetch_nri.py, which fails loudly and names any code that returns no",
    "row -- so a typo cannot reach the scoring engine as a hub that silently has",
    "no baseline exposure, which would read as 'safe'."
  ],
